From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: office <office@localhost>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 12:00:00 +0500
Subject: [PATCH] export: Word positioned pages keep each row's place, baseline
 and font

Owner report 2026-10-29: PDF to Word kept the text editable but moved it. On a page kept as positioned text boxes
(text overlapping a stamp or picture) every paragraph was one box exactly as wide as the PDF text, with no font, so the
reader's default font (Arial in our Docs, Calibri in Word) rewrapped it: rows ran onto the rows below, paragraph rows
were rewrapped at the reader's line height and the first baseline sat 3 pt low.
- edit: ReadingBlocks also returns the reading lines its blocks index into.
- engine: export blocks carry the font family and, for paragraphs of several visual rows, each row's text and box.
- export: `family()` maps a PDF font name to Word's family name; runs get w:rFonts. An upright positioned paragraph is one
  box with one paragraph per row, indented to the row's left edge, with an exact line height that puts each row's
  bottom (and so its baseline) where the page has it; the box reaches the page's right edge so a slightly wider
  substitute font does not wrap a row.
